{
  "dom_id": "step-q86231",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $2\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $9?$\n\nI. $[5]$\n\nII. $[2]$\n\nIII. $[19]$",
  "worked_solution": "We have $9\\in [x]$ if $9∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,|\\,(9-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $9\\in [5]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $9\\notin [2]$ because $2∤(9-2)\\,\\times $\n\n- $9\\in [19]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-19)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
